'''''Akitsushima'' (PLH-32)''' is a [[Shikishima-class patrol vessel|''Akitsushima''-class]] [[Patrol boat|patrol vessel]] currently operated by the [[Japanese Coast Guard]].

== Design ==
{{Main|Shikishima-class patrol vessel}}

This vessel is an enlarged and updated version of the earlier [[Japanese patrol vessel Shikishima|''Shikishima'']].{{Sfn|Kaijin-sha|2014}} Aviation facilities have also been enhanced: whereas ''Shikishima'' is carrying [[Eurocopter AS332 Super Puma|Eurocopter AS332]] helicopters, this vessel is able to carry the larger [[Eurocopter EC225 Super Puma|EC225LP Super Puma]].{{Sfn|Kaijin-sha|2014}}

Weapons have also been updated and enhanced.{{Sfn|Kaijin-sha|2014}} As for the large calibre autocanon, the vessel is equipped with two single-mounted [[Bofors 40 mm Automatic Gun L/70|Bofors L/70 40 mm guns]], as opposed to the double-mounted [[Oerlikon GDF|Oerlikon L/90 35mm guns]] of the ''Shikishima''. These autocanons are the same as those carried by the [[Aso-class patrol vessel|''Aso''-class]] and [[Hida-class patrol vessel|''Hida''-class]], and are capable of precision firing with an optical director.{{Sfn|Nakanomyo|2015}} The [[M61 Vulcan|JM61 20 mm]] guns are also upgraded to the production version as the other vessels, while the ''Shikishima'' is equipped with the earlier prototypes.{{Sfn|Nakanomyo|2015}}

== Construction and career ==
''Akitsushima'' was [[Keel laying|laid down]] on 10 May 2011 and [[Launched (ship)|launched]] on 4 July 2012 by [[IHI Corporation|IHI]], [[Tokyo]]. She was [[Commissioned (ship)|commissioned]] on 28 November 2013.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Large Patrol Vessel Akitsushima (PLH-32) Enters Japan's Coast Guard Service|url=http://www.navyrecognition.com/index.php/news/defence-news/year-2013-news/november-2013-navy-naval-forces-maritime-industry-technology-security-global-news/1391--large-patrol-vessel-akitsushima-plh-32-enters-japans-coast-guard-service-.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131202081453/http://www.navyrecognition.com/index.php/news/defence-news/year-2013-news/november-2013-navy-naval-forces-maritime-industry-technology-security-global-news/1391--large-patrol-vessel-akitsushima-plh-32-enters-japans-coast-guard-service-.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=December 2, 2013|access-date=2021-05-04|website=www.navyrecognition.com}}</ref>

In 2015, during the [[Emperor of Japan|Emperor]]'s and {{nihongo|[[Empress Michiko|Empress]]'s visit|[[:ja:行幸|行幸啓]]|Gyōkōkei}} to [[Palau]], ''Akitsushima'' was used as an accommodation ship. Slopes and handrails were set up so that both elderly Majesties could get on board comfortably.<ref>{{Cite news|author=Manabe Mitsuyuki|author2=Koseki Toshiki|date=2015-04-01|title=Accommodation "Akitsushima" To the details to be tailored to both Majesties|newspaper=[[Mainichi Shimbun]]|url=http://mainichi.jp/feature/koushitsu/news/20150401k0000m040158000c.html|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150404000600/http://mainichi.jp/feature/koushitsu/news/20150401k0000m040158000c.html|archive-date=2015-04-04}}</ref>

